  "headline_finding": {
    "what": "On CONFORMANCE, mean raw agreement is 0.88 while mean Cohen's kappa is -0.17 and the co-error rate is 0.46. High apparent consensus with NEGATIVE chance-corrected agreement means the agreement is an artifact of shared label bias, not convergence on the law.",
    "why_it_matters": "This is the exact case accuracy-only benchmarks cannot surface. Two models reported at 88% agreement look like mutual corroboration to a buyer or auditor. Corrected for chance they agree LESS than random raters would, and on 46% of items they are BOTH WRONG IN THE SAME DIRECTION \u2014 a second opinion with no safety margin.",
    "per_axis_kappa": {
      "governance": 0.17079523809523808,
      "safety": 0.251375,
      "provenance": 0.6,
      "conformance": -0.1702,
      "openness": 0.20002,
      "continuity": 0.10814
    },
    "prediction_scored": "I predicted 'high raw agreement and near-zero kappa' before the run. PARTIALLY RIGHT: kappa is near zero on four axes (+0.11 to +0.25 \u2014 'slight' on Landis-Koch). WRONG on provenance, where kappa is +0.60 ('moderate') \u2014 the models genuinely converge there. And I did not predict a NEGATIVE kappa anywhere; conformance at -0.17 was not anticipated.",
    "caveat": "n=11-24 per pair. These are directional, not resolved. Item discrimination is unmeasured."
  }
